# Git LFS is only for genuinely large binaries.
#
# *.keras is worth tracking in LFS (forward_model_v2_curve.keras is ~115 MB), though
# it is currently .gitignored and fetched from the Hub instead.
*.keras filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# NOTE: *.pkl was previously routed to LFS as well. That was a mistake and it is the
# reason a fresh `git clone` of this repo cannot run:
#
#   scaler_geo.pkl  -> 129-byte LFS pointer text (real file is 1575 bytes)
#   scaler_perf.pkl -> 129-byte LFS pointer text
#
# A git-based host (Hugging Face Space, Streamlit Community Cloud, any CI runner)
# clones without LFS, gets the pointer text, and joblib.load() fails. fly.io only
# works today because `fly deploy` uploads your local working tree, where LFS has
# already materialised the files.
#
# These scalers are ~1.6 KB -- four orders of magnitude below any LFS threshold --
# so they belong in git as ordinary files.
#
# TO FIX THE EXISTING POINTERS (one-time, on a machine with git-lfs installed):
#
#   git lfs install
#   git lfs pull                       # materialise the real bytes
#   git rm --cached scaler_geo.pkl scaler_perf.pkl
#   git add .gitattributes scaler_geo.pkl scaler_perf.pkl
#   git commit -m "store scalers as plain files, not LFS pointers"
#
# The Dockerfile now fails the build early if it ever sees a pointer again.
